    <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been presented since the 6th Academy Awards ceremony in 1934, making it one of the longest-running categories in Oscar history. Across <strong>93 consecutive ceremonies</strong> from 1934 to 2026, the award has recognized <strong>79 distinct composers</strong> for their work on <strong>92 unique winning films</strong>. (The single duplicate title, <em>Oppenheimer</em>, appears in both the 2023 and 2024 data entries, both credited to Ludwig Göransson.)</p>
    <p>The category has undergone name changes and occasional structural splits — notably, the Academy maintained separate awards for dramatic and comedy/musical scores during parts of the 1960s through the 1990s — but this dataset consolidates to one representative winner per ceremony year, providing a clean longitudinal view of how the industry's highest honor for film music has evolved.</p>
    <p>Of the 79 unique composers, <strong>17 have won more than once</strong>, accounting for 26 of the 93 ceremonies (28.0%). The remaining 62 composers were one-time winners. This ratio tells a central story: while the award is capable of crowning newcomers, a relatively small cohort of repeat winners has exerted disproportionate influence on the record books.</p>
