    <p>The career spans of these repeat winners are revealing. <strong>Hans Zimmer</strong> holds the longest gap between wins: <strong>27 years</strong> (The Lion King in 1994 to Dune in 2021). <strong>Maurice Jarre's</strong> gap of 19 years between Doctor Zhivago (1965) and A Passage to India (1984) and <strong>John Barry's</strong> 17-year gap (1968–1985) further illustrate how the best composers can remain competitive across eras. At the other extreme, <strong>Franz Waxman</strong> and <strong>Alan Menken</strong> each won in consecutive years — Waxman in 1950–1951 and Menken in 1991–1992 — while <strong>Gustavo Santaolalla</strong> achieved back-to-back wins in 2005–2006. Most recently, <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> won consecutive ceremonies in 2023 and 2024.</p>

