    <p>The <strong>1930s</strong> and <strong>1940s</strong> saw a high ratio of unique composers to ceremonies — 9 and 12 respectively — reflecting the early, still-consolidating nature of the award. The <strong>1950s</strong> mark the tightest concentration: only 7 unique composers across 10 ceremonies, driven by repeat wins from Dimitri Tiomkin (3), Franz Waxman (2), and the continued dominance of the Golden Age guard.</p>
    <p>The <strong>1980s</strong> stand out as the decade of maximum diversity: <strong>12 unique individual composers</strong> were credited across just 10 ceremonies. Every ceremony in the 1980s was won by a different lead composer — an unmatched streak that reflects the era's stylistic breadth (from Vangelis's synthesizers to Herbie Hancock's jazz to Ryuichi Sakamoto's world fusion).</p>
    <p>The <strong>2020s</strong>, though incomplete (7 of 10 ceremonies at time of analysis), already show signs of reconcentration: Ludwig Göransson accounts for 4 of 7 ceremonies, mirroring patterns of dominance not seen since the 1950s.</p>

