  <!-- ════════ §4: ERA ANALYSIS ════════ -->
  <section id="era-analysis">
    <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    <p>Grouping the 93 ceremonies into four broad eras reveals how the character of Oscar-winning scores — and the composers who create them — has shifted over time.</p>

    <div class="era-grid">
      <div class="era-card golden">
        <h4>Golden Age (1934–1959)</h4>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>26</strong> ceremonies &middot; <strong>25</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>6</strong> repeat ceremonies</div>
        <p>The era of grand orchestral romanticism. <strong>Max Steiner</strong> (3 wins) and <strong>Dimitri Tiomkin</strong> (3 wins) were the defining figures, alongside dual-winner <strong>Franz Waxman</strong>. Winning scores were overwhelmingly symphonic, serving sweeping historical epics (<em>Ben-Hur</em>), frontier dramas (<em>Stagecoach</em>), and psychological films (<em>Spellbound</em>). Many winners were European-born émigré composers who brought classical training to Hollywood.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card silver">
        <h4>Silver Age (1960–1979)</h4>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>20</strong> ceremonies &middot; <strong>18</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>7</strong> repeat ceremonies</div>
        <p>A transitional period that saw the orchestral tradition persist while pop, jazz, and electronic textures began to infiltrate. <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> bookended epic scores (Lawrence of Arabia, Doctor Zhivago) while <strong>John Williams</strong> closed the era with the blockbuster scores for <em>Jaws</em> and <em>Star Wars</em>. The period also produced outliers like <strong>Burt Bacharach</strong> (pop-influenced) and <strong>Giorgio Moroder</strong> (electronic), signaling a future of stylistic plurality.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card modern">
        <h4>Modern Era (1980–1999)</h4>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>20</strong> ceremonies &middot; <strong>18</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>7</strong> repeat ceremonies</div>
        <p>Peak stylistic diversity. The 1980s produced ten distinct lead composers — from <strong>Vangelis</strong>'s synthesizers (<em>Chariots of Fire</em>) to <strong>Herbie Hancock</strong>'s jazz (<em>Round Midnight</em>) to collaborative world-music scores (<em>The Last Emperor</em>). The 1990s saw <strong>Alan Menken</strong> briefly dominate Disney's animation renaissance with 3 wins in 4 years, while <strong>Hans Zimmer</strong> and <strong>John Williams</strong> continued to prove orchestral scoring's enduring appeal. The era's internationalism was notable: winners included Italian, French, Greek, and Japanese composers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card contemporary">
        <h4>Contemporary (2000–2026)</h4>
        <div class="era-stat"><strong>27</strong> ceremonies &middot; <strong>22</strong> unique composers &middot; <strong>6</strong> repeat ceremonies</div>
        <p>The most globally diverse era, with winners from India (<strong>A.R. Rahman</strong>), China (<strong>Tan Dun</strong>), Argentina (<strong>Gustavo Santaolalla</strong>), Italy (<strong>Ennio Morricone</strong>), Germany (<strong>Volker Bertelmann</strong>), and Iceland (<strong>Hildur Guðnadóttir</strong> — the first solo female winner in 2019). Electronic and hybrid scores gained legitimacy (<strong>Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross</strong>), while <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> emerged as the era's most dominant figure with 4 wins by age 42. The late Contemporary period shows signs of reconcentration: Göransson won 4 of the final 9 ceremonies in this dataset.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

