    <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>

    <ul class="milestone-list">
      <li><strong>First winner:</strong> Louis Silvers for <em>One Night of Love</em> (1934, 6th Academy Awards), establishing the category's inaugural benchmark.</li>
      <li><strong>Most recent winner:</strong> Ludwig Göransson for <em>Sinners</em> (2026, 98th Academy Awards) — his 4th win, tying the all-time record.</li>
      <li><strong>Largest collaborative credit:</strong> The 1939 award for <em>Stagecoach</em> was shared among four composers: Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold, and Leo Shuken.</li>
      <li><strong>First female solo winner:</strong> Hildur Guðnadóttir for <em>Joker</em> (2019) — a landmark moment for gender representation in the category after 85 years.</li>
      <li><strong>Longest gap between wins:</strong> Hans Zimmer — <strong>27 years</strong> between <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) and <em>Dune</em> (2021), the longest drought-to-win span in category history.</li>
      <li><strong>Fastest to 4 wins:</strong> Ludwig Göransson reached 4 wins spanning 2018–2026 (8 years). By comparison, John Williams's 4 wins spanned 18 years (1975–1993) and John Barry's spanned 24 years (1966–1990).</li>
      <li><strong>Consecutive-year wins:</strong> Achieved by Franz Waxman (1950–51), Alan Menken (1991–92), Gustavo Santaolalla (2005–06), and Ludwig Göransson (2023–24).</li>
      <li><strong>Same film, consecutive ceremonies:</strong> <em>Oppenheimer</em> is the only film title to appear in two consecutive ceremony years in this dataset (2023 and 2024), both credited to Ludwig Göransson.</li>
      <li><strong>Posthumous recognition:</strong> Victor Young's win for <em>Around the World in 80 Days</em> (1956) came after decades of nominations — it was his first and only win.</li>
      <li><strong>Late-career triumph:</strong> Ennio Morricone won for <em>The Hateful Eight</em> (2015) — his first competitive Oscar after a legendary career spanning over 500 film and television scores.</li>
    </ul>
