  <!-- ════════ §6: CONCLUSIONS ════════ -->
  <section id="conclusions">
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

    <div class="takeaway-box">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The award rewards longevity — but speed of accumulation is increasing.</strong>
          Historically, reaching 3+ wins required a career spanning decades (Max Steiner: 10 years, John Barry: 24 years, John Williams: 18 years). Ludwig Göransson's path to 4 wins in just 8 years (2018–2026) suggests the contemporary Academy may concentrate its favor more rapidly once a composer enters its orbit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Diversity peaked in the 1980s — and may not return soon.</strong>
          The 1980s' record of 12 distinct credited composers in 10 ceremonies remains unmatched. The trend in the 2020s is moving in the opposite direction: Göransson's 4 wins in 7 ceremonies represents a 57% dominance rate, the highest of any composer in any decade.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stylistic boundaries have permanently expanded.</strong>
          From Vangelis's synthesizers (1981) through Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross's electronic scores (2010, 2020) to A.R. Rahman's Bollywood-inflected work (2008), the category has moved decisively beyond its symphonic origins. Today, there is no single "Oscar style" — genre versatility is itself the winning trait.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Global representation is the defining trend of the 21st century.</strong>
          The Contemporary era has seen winners from more countries and musical traditions than any previous period. Hildur Guðnadóttir's 2019 win as the first solo female composer adds a gender-diversity milestone to this trajectory.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Watch the reconcentration signal.</strong>
          The late 2020s face a key question: will Göransson's dominance continue, or will the Academy's pattern of eventually rotating favor produce a new wave of first-time winners? Historical precedent suggests that concentrated periods (1950s Tiomkin/Steiner, 1960s Barry/Jarre) are typically followed by diversification — but the 2020s have not yet shown that inflection.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
